{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":234275,"duration_api_ms":232011,"num_turns":27,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"adf0ee22-0b2b-41c1-b90a-7060ddc546aa","total_cost_usd":1.6588469999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":124496,"cache_read_input_tokens":1261784,"output_tokens":9991,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":124496,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":9991,"cacheReadInputTokens":1261784,"cacheCreationInputTokens":124496,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.6588469999999997,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `algae_growth.rs:200-204`, the `.min(periphyton_capacity_g)` cap on periphyton biomass silently destroys N and C when biomass exceeds capacity (e.g., after a capacity reduction from parameter changes). The discarded biomass is not routed to any dissolved-organic pool. Route the excess to `route_algae_loss_to_dissolved_organics` so the N/C is not lost. Concretely: compute `let excess_g = (periphyton_new_uncapped - periphyton_capacity_g).max(0.0);`, apply the `.min()`, then call `route_algae_loss_to_dissolved_organics(state, excess_g, n_to_c_ratio);`.","rationale":"This is a real mass-conservation leak. While `surface_cap_factor` suppresses growth near capacity, the cap can still trim nonzero biomass when capacity shrinks (e.g., reduced colonizable area from substrate changes). The budget guard tolerance of 1e-6 mg won't catch small per-tick losses, but they accumulate over long simulations. The acceptance criteria require 'closed-system ticks show near-zero net delta', and this path violates that when it fires."},{"body":"In `engine.rs:353-366`, `CleanFilter` destroys microbe biomass (`*= 1.0 - setback`) without routing the N/C to any output pool. The biomass just disappears. Route the removed biomass to detritus (via `live_biomass_detrital_mass_g` → `fine_detritus_g_total`) or dissolved organics (via `route_live_biomass_to_dissolved_organics`) so the export is mass-conserving. Alternatively, if the intent is that cleaning physically removes biomass from the tank (down the drain), document this clearly and ensure the budget entry shows an explicit export amount rather than relying on snapshot diff.","rationale":"While `CleanFilter` is correctly flagged as an open-system action in `tick_has_closed_system_nitrogen`, the downstream dependents (tanksim-6e5.1.2.2 and tanksim-6e5.3.4) will audit N/C bookkeeping and expect each export to be explicit and traceable. Currently the budget entry for clean_filter shows N/C vanishing into a snapshot diff without any explicit in/out accounting. Either route the biomass to a real pool or add an explicit budget delta (like water_change does) so the audit trail is clear."},{"body":"The `test_budget_component_labels_cover_all_element_bearing_fields` test catches new N/C fields by convention (`_mg_n_total`, `_mg_c_total`, `biomass_g`, etc.) but would not catch a field named with a non-standard suffix (e.g., `organic_matter_g`, `nitrogen_pool_mg`). Add a brief doc comment on `nitrogen_budget_components` and `carbon_budget_components` stating the naming convention and the obligation to update these functions when adding new element-bearing fields to TankState. The acceptance criteria note 'Consider a compile-time or test-time check that all biomass-bearing state fields are included' — the current test provides this, but the convention it relies on needs to be documented at the declaration site of the budget component functions.","rationale":"The reviewer comments explicitly call out that 'Missing a pool in these helpers would mask conservation bugs' and suggest 'a compile-time or test-time check'. The test exists and is well-designed, but its effectiveness depends on future contributors knowing the naming convention. A doc comment at the definition site of the two component functions closes the discoverability gap."}],"summary":"The budget tracking implementation is solid and well-tested. BudgetLedger, BudgetDelta, and per-system snapshot tracking work correctly. The canonical total_nitrogen()/total_carbon() helpers are defined in one place and include all current pools (dissolved, substrate, biomass, detritus). Conservation tests pass for 24–72 tick closed-system runs covering nitrogen, carbon, plant trimming, shrimp mortality, and shrimp reproduction. The zero-cost-when-disabled requirement is met via Option<BudgetLedger>. The field-coverage test using serialization + convention-based path matching is a clever compile-time-adjacent guard. I found three issues: (1) periphyton capacity cap silently destroys N/C without routing, (2) CleanFilter destroys microbe biomass without routing or explicit export tracking, and (3) the naming convention that the coverage test relies on is not documented at the budget component function definitions."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"03c21a51-3b4f-4e62-a3cb-747312a7496d"}
